For forms with multiple fields, a single error-summary block at the top — in addition to (not instead of) each field's own inline error — gives a screen reader user a fast overview and a direct way to jump to each problem:

```html
<div role="alert" aria-live="assertive" tabindex="-1" id="error-summary">
  <h2>2 errors found</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="#email">Email is required</a></li>
    <li><a href="#password">Password must be at least 8 characters</a></li>
  </ul>
</div>
```

```js
form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
  if (!isValid()) {
    e.preventDefault();
    renderErrorSummary();
    document.getElementById('error-summary').focus(); // moves focus here explicitly
  }
});
```

This pattern combines two techniques deliberately: `role="alert"` plus `aria-live="assertive"` for the immediate announcement (justified here since a failed submission is exactly the urgent case assertive is for), and an explicit `.focus()` call, using the block's own `tabindex="-1"`, so a keyboard user's next Tab press starts from the summary — landing them right where they need to be, rather than wherever they happened to submit from.
